  <p>“We would like live music during the ceremony” sounds precise until the first timing question arrives. How long is the entrance? Does the registry office allow a complete piece? Who signals the exit? What happens when a reading is shortened or the rings take four minutes longer than planned?</p>
  <p>The answer is not a minute-perfect running order. It is a small set of musical decisions plus a fallback language that the performer, venue and one designated coordinator all understand.</p>

  <h2>Choose moments before choosing songs</h2>
  <p>Start with the function of the music. A ceremony commonly offers four distinct jobs:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Arrival:</strong> create a settled atmosphere while guests take their places.</li>
    <li><strong>Entrance:</strong> shape attention and support movement.</li>
    <li><strong>Reflection:</strong> hold space during signing, a ritual or a quiet pause.</li>
    <li><strong>Exit or reception:</strong> release the formal tension and carry people into the next part of the day.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>You may need only two. A short civil ceremony can feel crowded when every transition becomes a performance slot. Let the ceremony breathe.</p>

  <h2>Build a timing range, not a stopwatch</h2>
  <p>Give each musical moment a minimum and maximum useful length. The performer can then prepare an ending point or repeatable section. “Entrance: 70–120 seconds, end when both partners are in place” is operational. “Play this 3:42 recording exactly” is not.</p>
  <p>For a requested song, ask whether it works as a solo viola arrangement and whether a shortened form still feels complete. Melody-led pieces usually adapt more naturally than productions whose identity depends on drums, bass and studio texture.</p>

  <h2>Name the person who gives the cue</h2>
  <p>The couple should not be managing playback logic on the day. Choose one coordinator — planner, celebrant, registrar or trusted guest — and introduce that person to the performer before guests arrive. Agree on visible cues for start, continue and finish.</p>
  <p>For entrances, the most reliable stop signal is often a physical event: the last person reaches a marked place, the doors close, or the officiant turns. That survives schedule drift better than a timestamp.</p>

  <h2>Confirm the room and practical conditions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Is the ceremony indoors, outdoors or moving between both?</li>
    <li>Is there a dry, level place for the performer and instrument?</li>
    <li>Will amplification be necessary, and who supplies power and a sound system?</li>
    <li>Can the instrument remain safely on site between ceremony and reception?</li>
    <li>Does the venue or registry office limit music duration, volume or setup time?</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Outdoor plans need a weather fallback that protects the instrument, not only the guests. “Under a small umbrella” is not an adequate plan for rain, wind or direct summer heat.</p>

  <h2>A one-page brief is enough</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Example of useful detail</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Venue</td><td>Exact room or outdoor area, access time and contact</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Musical moments</td><td>Arrival, entrance, signing, exit</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Repertoire</td><td>Confirmed pieces plus one flexible fallback</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cues</td><td>Who signals and what the visible signal is</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Timing</td><td>Useful range, not only a scheduled start</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Technical</td><td>Acoustic/amplified, power, shelter and setup window</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Where a real performer page helps</h2>
  <p>If you want to see how a specialist frames repertoire, ceremony moments and requested songs, the <a href="https://kim-marie-borger.com/hochzeiten/">wedding-music information from violist Kim Marie Borger</a> is a useful concrete example. Use it to prepare better questions; the final plan still needs to be agreed for your venue and date.</p>
  <p>The goal is not to make live music behave like a fixed audio file. Its value is the opposite: a musician can respond to the pace of the room. A good brief protects that flexibility.</p>
